SOLUTIONS={
1:('DIG-01','Compare distances from 208: 190 is 18 away, 200 is 8 away, 205 is 3 away, 210 is 2 away and 218 is 10 away. The smallest distance is 2, so choose 210.'),
2:('RATE-01','Add the birthday money: $47 + $25 = $47 + $20 + $5 = $72.'),
3:('DIV-01','A multiple of 8 is an exact whole-number product with 8. Since 8 × 6 = 48, choose 48. The neighbouring multiples are 40 and 56, so 58 is not a multiple.'),
4:('GEO-04','Turning the necklace over reverses the bead order and reflects each letter. The back view reads mirrored E,T,A,K from left to right. A and T look unchanged under this reflection, but E and K must face backwards. Only A shows the whole reflection correctly.'),
5:('DIG-01','Eight thousand is 8000, eight hundred is 800 and eight is 8. Their total is 8808. The tens digit is zero.'),
6:('DIV-02','Their shared amount must be a positive common multiple of 20c and 50c. The smallest is 100c. Jane needs five 20c coins and Tariq two 50c coins: 5 + 2 = 7 coins altogether.'),
7:('DIV-03','Four full shelves hold 4 × 12 = 48 books. The remainder is 58 − 48 = 10, so the last shelf has 10 books.'),
8:('SEQ-01','Add successive row sizes: 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6 + 7 = 28. Thus the display has 7 levels. Six levels would use only 21 cups.'),
9:('DATA-03','Longer time means farther right on the horizontal axis; lower cost means lower on the vertical axis. Bill is to the right of Pat and below Pat, so Bill’s call lasted longer but cost less.'),
10:('ALG-04','Convert all clues to present ages. Aimee is 8; Bilal is 9 − 3 = 6; Caitlin is 5 + 2 = 7. From youngest to oldest: Bilal, Caitlin, Aimee.'),
11:('DATA-03','Read the equal scale intervals between 36 and 38. The short ticks are 0.2 apart, with the centre long mark at 37. The pointer lies halfway between 37.2 and 37.4, giving 37.3.'),
12:('SPATIAL-03','The target has 20 unit squares. Shape A has five squares, so four copies can cover it. Follow each bent five-square strip around the border: flipping and rotating A lets its two end squares fit the top and bottom protrusions while the middle runs around the frame. The shape that fits these turns without gaps or overlaps is A.'),
13:('RATE-01','Library runs from 11:30 am until 12:30 pm. Clock A shows 11:45, inside that interval. The others show 9:00, 1:30, 12:45 and 11:15, all outside the Library lesson.'),
14:('DATA-03','Alan worked 5 × 48 = 240 days. The circle is marked in twelve equal portions, and bus occupies five of them. Each portion represents 240 ÷ 12 = 20 days, so bus accounts for 5 × 20 = 100 days.'),
15:('LOGIC-03','The sum of row counts must equal the sum of column counts, which rules out B,C,D. In A, two columns each contain three counters and the third contains none, forcing every row to contain two, contrary to its labels. E works: fill all three cells of row 1, the last two cells of row 2, and the last cell of row 3. Rows then contain 3,2,1 and columns 1,2,3.'),
16:('RATE-01','The first 10 kg costs $24. The remaining 18 kg needs four charged blocks of up to 5 kg, because even a partial block costs $8. Extra cost is 4 × $8 = $32. Total: $24 + $32 = $56.'),
17:('LOGIC-02','Each of the four triangles must contain 1,2,3, so adding their totals gives 24. This counts each inner-square vertex twice and each outer vertex once. To minimise the sum of all eight labels, maximise the inner-square total. Adjacent inner vertices differ, so the largest possible inner labels alternate 2,3,2,3, summing to 10. All outer vertices can then be 1. The overall total is 10 + 4 = 14; equivalently 24 − 10 = 14.'),
18:('GEO-02','Draw the six radii of the regular hexagon to divide it into six equal equilateral triangles. The two unshaded side triangles each have the area of one of these units. Four of the six units remain shaded, so the fraction is 4/6 = 2/3.'),
19:('LOGIC-03','The first row gives four apples = 24, so an apple is 6. The second gives 12 + two bananas = 22, so a banana is 5. Column 1 gives 6 + 6 + 5 + pear = 21, so a pear is 4. Column 2 then gives 6 + 6 + orange + 4 = 19, so an orange is 3. Apple plus orange is 6 + 3 = 9.'),
20:('RATE-02','Two big rooms take the time of three small rooms. Therefore one big plus three small rooms takes the time of three big rooms, which is 90 minutes. One big takes 30 minutes and one small 20. Three big plus six small take 3 × 30 + 6 × 20 = 210 minutes = 3½ hours.'),
21:('GEO-02','The rectangle has area 3 × 4 = 12 cm². Each unshaded corner triangle has legs 3 cm and 2 cm, after subtracting the labelled 1 cm portions. Each has area ½ × 3 × 2 = 3 cm². Subtracting both leaves 6 cm² shaded, which is 6/12 = 1/2.'),
22:('COUNT-01','Anh takes positions 1,5,9,13,17: five coins, leaving 15. Brenda takes positions 1,4,7,10,13 in the new shortened row: five, leaving 10. Chen takes alternate coins from those ten: five, leaving five for Dimitris. All four get five coins.'),
23:('SOLID-01','The small tank’s volume is 5 × 3 × 4 = 60 m³. The large tank’s volume is 12 × 6 × 10 = 720 m³, twelve times as large. At the same flow rate, filling time also multiplies by twelve: 2 × 12 = 24 hours.'),
24:('SPATIAL-04','The distinct fence lengths total 8 + 8 + 6 + 6 + 4 × 5 = 48 km. All four corner junctions have odd degree, so a closed inspection route must repeat paths pairing those corners. The cheapest pairing repeats the two 6 km sides, adding 12 km; the other pairings cost at least 16 or 20. With those two sides repeated every junction has even degree, so a closed route exists. Minimum distance: 48 + 12 = 60 km.'),
25:('DIG-04','Borrow across the lower ten digits. The result consists of nine 1s, then a 0, then nine 8s, then a 9. For a smaller analogue, 111111 − 222 = 110889. The required digit sum is 9 × 1 + 0 + 9 × 8 + 9 = 90.')}
